Parameter data used by the core packages is drawn from published sources, and every value carries
a citation at the point of use. docs/sources/bibliography.md is the complete list of those
sources. The four below supply the segment parameters, the body dimensions, the joint coordinate
conventions and the lower-limb reference model, and none of them restricts redistribution:

  - de Leva, P. (1996). Adjustments to Zatsiorsky-Seluyanov's segment inertia parameters.
    Journal of Biomechanics 29(9), 1223-1230.
  - Gordon, C. C., et al. (2014). 2012 Anthropometric Survey of U.S. Army Personnel (ANSUR II).
    US Army Natick Soldier RD&E Center. US federal government work.
  - Wu, G., et al. (2002, 2005). ISB recommendations on joint coordinate system definitions.
    Journal of Biomechanics 35(4) and 38(5).
  - Rajagopal, A., et al. (2016). Full-body musculoskeletal model for muscle-driven simulation of
    human gait. IEEE TBME 63(10), 2068-2079. Model freely distributed via SimTK.

Two more sources of muscle parameters carry a licence of their own. Their values are used under
it, with this attribution and a citation at each value:

  - caggiano2022: Caggiano, V., et al. (2022). MyoSuite -- A contact-rich simulation suite for
    musculoskeletal motor control. PMLR (L4DC). The models are Apache-2.0; see below.
  - seth2019: Seth, A., et al. (2019). Muscle contributions to upper-extremity movement and work
    from a musculoskeletal model of the human shoulder. Frontiers in Neurorobotics 13, 90. CC BY.

Reference models, vendored under tools/validate-external/ for the validation of spec section
13.6. Parts of them are also distributed, as stated here:

  - MyoSuite myo_sim (MyoHub) - Apache-2.0. The copy is pinned at commit eb327acb (the 0.2.2
    release), and its licence is at tools/validate-external/myo_sim/LICENSE. That directory's
    README says how to verify the pin. Four things from it are distributed:
      - Its scalar muscle parameters: peak isometric force, optimal fibre length and tendon
        slack length. The tools/cli/bin/generate-*-muscles.mjs generators that read it extract
        them into packages/muscle-data, cited caggiano2022.
      - Its muscle via points, carried into this skeleton's bone frames by
        tools/cli/bin/generate-muscle-via-points.mjs as packages/skeleton/src/muscleViaPoints.ts.
      - The muscle paths extracted from it, apps/studio/public/sourceSites.json, which the
        studio serves for the Align tab.
      - Its bone meshes, which every studio build ships in refMeshes/ for the Align tab. The
        licence file ships beside them, as refMeshes/LICENSE.

Third-party software dependencies:

  - MuJoCo (Google DeepMind) - Apache-2.0
  - three.js - MIT

The Data tier of ADR-009 is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. It is:

  - packages/skeleton: bone placement, landmarks, frames, joint centres, and everything derived
    from them.
  - packages/assets-anatomical: the bone geometry and measurements under its data/ directory.
  - packages/muscle-data: the muscle definitions, whose attachments, length ranges and some fibre
    lengths are measured on that skeleton.
  - packages/scenarios: the fixtures under its data/ directory. The rest of that package is code
    and stays Apache-2.0.

Each of the first three packages holds the licence text in its LICENSE and says what it derives
from in its NOTICE; the scenario fixtures are covered by this file, and the licence text is the
same one. The geometry and everything measured on it derives from:

  - Z-Anatomy (z-anatomy.com, github.com/Z-Anatomy) - CC BY-SA 4.0
  - BodyParts3D, Database Center for Life Science, Japan - CC BY-SA 2.1 JP

Code packages remain Apache-2.0: they are not derivatives of the data they load. CONTRIBUTING.md
lists every package with its tier. See ADR-009 and ADR-011. No non-commercially-licensed material enters distributed data, because a non-commercial
licence cannot be combined with CC BY-SA in one work.
